{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TMStrxKPquxqh4hRxKZsq9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# The MBA Transformed? AI and Beyond â€” Symposium Summary\n2\t### Columbia Business School Â· New York, NY Â· June 1, 2026\n3\t\n4\tA one-day symposium on how generative and agentic AI are reshaping business-school teaching â€” the case method, analytics, entrepreneurship, assessment, and the infrastructure behind it all. This document summarizes every session, in program order. Each summary is grounded in the speaker's own recording and slides.\n5\t\n6\t> **About these summaries.** They are drawn from verified transcripts of the session recordings: each talk was transcribed with Whisper large-v3 and independently cross-checked against Vimeo's auto-captions (â‰ˆ92â€“98% agreement across all talks), with proper nouns reconciled against the speakers' slides. Full recordings, slides, and transcripts accompany this document in the archive.\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## The big picture â€” five threads that ran through the day\n11\t\n12\t1. **The case method is becoming interactive.** Instead of reading a static PDF, students now *talk to* AI â€” debating an AI interlocutor (Wang's CAiSEY), consulting AI-played characters in a living simulation (Martin; Maclean's HopeWorks), or being examined by a voice agent (Ipeirotis).\n13\t2. **\"Agents are business processes.\"** Several speakers reframed agentic AI as systems-design and process-thinking rather than a tool â€” and argued the durable skill is deciding *where the human sits* in the workflow (Lobel & Jagabathula; Canetti).\n14\t3. **Vibe coding makes everyone a builder.** Across many talks, MBAs build real, deployed apps with Lovable, Claude Code, Codex, Replit, Streamlit and Excel add-ins â€” shifting the skill from *writing* code to *specifying, verifying and iterating* (Broadie, Guetta, Boussioux, Shumsky, LaSala, Griffel).\n15\t4. **Assessment is in crisis â€” and being reinvented.** When written work no longer proves thinking, faculty turn to AI-run oral exams (Ipeirotis), discernment exercises (Lee), honor-system \"AI-off\" quizzes (Shumsky) and dialogue-as-assignment platforms (Beasley's Socrat).\n16\t5. **AI commoditizes \"good enough,\" so teaching must shift to differentiation and judgment.** A recurring thesis (sharpest in Toubia & Ben Sliman): since anyone can now produce adequate work, courses must teach students to differentiate themselves, exercise discernment, and do the things AI still can't.\n17\t\n18\t---\n19\t\n20\t## Morning\n21\t\n22\t### 9:10â€“10:00 Â· Generative AI and the case method\n23\t\n24\t#### Dan Wang, Columbia Business School\n25\t**What it was about:** A demo of **CAiSEY** (caisey.me), a voice-first AI tool that immerses students in real-time discussion to deepen critical thinking and prepare them for case-method classes.\n26\t- Live demo had a colleague role-play a \"mediocre\" student debating Netflix's content strategy (original vs. third-party licensing) against the AI.\n27\t- CAiSEY uses an OpenAI voice model plus a system prompt supporting debate, brainstorm, and role-play modes, customized per course with instructor questions, materials, and custom instructions.\n28\t- Scaled from a Spring 2025 pilot (275 students, 1 course) to **4,100+ students across 40+ courses and ~23â€“25 institutions**; valued as motivating, practical, and inclusive (ESL students, dyslexia/dysgraphia).\n29\t- An under-review paper found voice discussions show greater semantic diversity than text (an indicator of cognitive flexibility); students start in text but migrate to voice.\n30\t\n31\t**Notable takeaway:** CAiSEY aims not to make learning more efficient but to add productive *friction* so students still struggle toward their own ideas.\n32\t\n33\t#### SÃ©bastien Martin, Kellogg School of Management\n34\t**What it was about:** Reimagining the case study as an **interactive simulation** where students talk directly to AI-role-played characters instead of reading a static PDF.\n35\t- Demo of an operations case on a school-district transportation budget crisis, where students consult characters like a superintendent and a CFO â€” each a custom GPT with role-specific prompts and its own data, who disagree with one another.\n36\t- The format is adaptive, trains students to figure out the problem themselves, and is easy to edit via AI.\n37\t- Spreading at Kellogg into negotiation, boardroom (multi-AI), and data-analysis classes; a ~60-instructor cross-school team is running an RCT to measure impact.\n38\t\n39\t**Notable takeaway:** It's never been easier for a professor to design a \"simulation,\" but the payoff hinges entirely on how deliberately the instructor sets up the learning experience.\n40\t\n41\t### 10:00â€“10:25 Â· Teaching Agentic AI\n42\t\n43\t#### Ilan Lobel & Srikanth Jagabathula, NYU Stern\n44\t**What it was about:** How Stern built **\"Foundations of AI Agents,\"** a hands-on core course in its Tech MBA teaching systems-level thinking about agentic workflows.\n45\t- Half-semester (six weeks, 3 hrs/week); first taught Spring 2026 with ~60 students and 120 waitlisted, scaling to 8 sections.\n46\t- Two principles: **MBAs must be builders** (prototype, no-code) and **agents are business processes**; the arc runs Build â†’ Architect â†’ User-facing â†’ Enterprise/RAG â†’ Deploy â†’ Demo.\n47\t- Teaching platform is **n8n** (a visual pedagogical device, \"like Excel for optimization\"); **Lovable** for front-ends.\n48\t- Two lessons: focus on **where the human sits** (guardrail vs. bottleneck; automation vs. augmentation); and **\"don't do demos\"** â€” flashy demos bored students and failed the test of durable skill.\n49\t\n50\t**Notable takeaway:** Teaching AI *agents* differs from teaching AI â€” the durable skill is systems-level process thinking, especially deciding where humans belong in the workflow.\n51\t\n52\t### 10:40â€“12:20 Â· GenAI in Business â€” what it is, how it works, and how it can be used\n53\t\n54\t#### Olivier Toubia & Malek Ben Sliman, Columbia Business School\n55\t**What it was about:** Lessons from teaching **\"Generative AI for Business\"** since fall 2023, organized around using GenAI as a source of *differentiation* rather than commoditization.\n56\t- Serves four personas (users, consultants, developers, investors); the largest segment was tool users.\n57\t- Structured as a tree: roots (foundations and the \"dark side\"), trees (building tools, incl. Python and deployment), forest (ecosystem, monetization, managing GenAI).\n58\t- Core thesis: GenAI commoditizes \"good enough\" labor, so students must differentiate **vertically** (be the best with AI) and **horizontally** (be unique â€” build an \"AI identity\").\n59\t- Students build a personal **RAG \"MBA Copilot\"** in ~30 minutes (300+ built) with GitHub/Pinecone/Vercel/GPT-4o, and contribute to \"The A(I) List,\" a crowdsourced tool-review platform.\n60\t\n61\t**Notable takeaway:** Since anyone can now produce \"good enough\" work, the mission is teaching students to use GenAI to *differentiate themselves* so people still pay for their work.\n62\t\n63\t#### Rama Ramakrishnan, MIT Sloan\n64\t**What it was about:** Five years teaching **\"Hands-On Deep Learning\" (HODL)**, a code-first deep-learning elective for Sloan MBAs.\n65\t- Philosophy: \"light on math, heavy on intuition,\" with students coding models themselves rather than calling a \"techie friend.\"\n66\t- Wildly popular: the Spring 2026 section had the highest enrollment of all Sloan electives (170 registered, 100 waitlisted); the 2024 edition is on MIT OpenCourseWare.\n67\t- Mixes slides, live coding in Google Colab, and unscripted live demos (e.g., students try to fool an image classifier).\n68\t- Curriculum evolved to make **Transformers the platform**, adding LLM fine-tuning, RAG, agents-from-scratch, and diffusion models while dropping CNNs.\n69\t\n70\t**Notable takeaway:** Teach MBAs \"one level deeper than you think they need\" so they can do hard things and earn credibility with engineers.\n71\t\n72\t#### Daniel Guetta, Columbia Business School\n73\t**What it was about:** A live demo of **XLKitLearn v2**, an Excel add-in that lets MBAs use advanced GenAI (LLMs, embeddings, agents) without learning to code from scratch.\n74\t- Chose Excel over vibe coding to preserve structured, computer-like thinking (\"dragging a formula down\" teaches a loop without naming it).\n75\t- Brings LLMs into Excel via `xkl.llm`, running prompts across multiple models with per-call cost reporting and guardrail comparisons; embeddings via `xkl.embed` (text is \"closest\" to its own author).\n76\t- Agent tools (\"batteries included\"): send/receive texts, flight search, stock prices, and `runPython` in a sandbox; a server back-end manages per-student keys, quotas, and prompt caching for cost control.\n77\t\n78\t**Notable takeaway:** \"There is so much more to AI than a chatbot\" â€” Excel can turn non-coders into builders of embeddings- and agent-driven applications.\n79\t\n80\t#### Kyle Jensen, Yale School of Management *(slides only â€” not recorded)*\n81\t**What it was about:** A fast, technical tour of **infrastructure for teaching with AI** â€” how to run your own teaching stack so agents (and students) have power without chaos.\n82\t- **Write your own Canvas** (most universities disable the Canvas API; it's now \"vibe-codable\") for agent grading, point-in-time backups, and easy safety controls.\n83\t- **GitHub Classroom + the `gh` CLI** for agent-friendly assignment distribution and auto-grading; **gateways/OpenRouter** for per-student LLM keys, budgets, and request inspection.\n84\t- **Skills** (\"a Claude Code marketplace\") to give student/AI pairs *your taste* by default; **Ansible/OpenTofu** for reproducible infrastructure-as-code; **Tailscale** for private services.\n85\t- **Meilisearch / full-text search** as \"the killer enabler\" of agentic AI (\"RAG is dead; use AI as a reasoning machine, not a fact database\"); **Playwright-CLI** to smoke-test apps; **guard models** to police inputs/outputs and redact PII.\n86\t\n87\t**Notable takeaway:** Own your teaching infrastructure â€” full-text search plus agent-friendly tooling beats bolting a chatbot onto someone else's LMS.\n88\t\n89\t### 12:20â€“12:50 Â· Mini-presentations â€” Part 1\n90\t\n91\t#### Mark Broadie, Columbia Business School â€” \"Using lovable.dev in Teaching\"\n92\t**What it was about:** Using **Lovable** (an AI website builder) to teach the full analytics process â€” get data, build model, present results â€” and, for the first time, how to *manage a team of data scientists*.\n93\t- Lovable writes scraping code for live data, writes analytical functions (replacing Excel add-ins), and builds interactive, hosted dashboards (React/Tailwind + Supabase, one-click publish, code you own).\n94\t- A single naive prompt gets surprisingly far but is \"garbage\"; the real skill is **iterative refinement** to a polished product, then saving the final prompt as a reusable \"skill.\"\n95\t- Examples: an NFL win-probability chart \"better than ESPN,\" NCAA-regionals map, sortable radar charts.\n96\t- Stresses **verification**: build test suites (e.g., match R `glm` / Python `statsmodels` to 3 decimals) since students assume AI output is right.\n97\t\n98\t**Notable takeaway:** Lovable reaches the presentation/communication stage Excel never could, letting students learn to specify, verify, and iterate like managers of data scientists.\n99\t\n100\t#### Luke Beasley, Meta â€” \"Socrat: AI-Powered Learning for the Modern Classroom\"\n101\t**What it was about:** **Socrat** turns AI dialogue itself into trackable assignments and gives instructors a data feedback loop on student understanding.\n102\t- Addresses the \"data opportunity cost\": students already have hundreds of isolated AI conversations where struggling students stay invisible.\n103\t- Instructors set up a class in minutes by uploading materials, yielding an LMS-style homepage and a class-grounded tutor.\n104\t- Assignment types where *dialogue is the assignment*: Socratic dialogue, debate, teach-back, source analysis â€” each with an instructor-designed reasoning path.\n105\t- A data layer surfaces strengths/weaknesses at assignment, student, and class levels.\n106\t\n107\t**Notable takeaway:** When AI dialogue becomes the assignment in one closed ecosystem, instructors finally see how every student thinks â€” including those who never raise a hand.\n108\t\n109\t#### Thomas Lee, UC Berkeley Haas â€” \"Teaching Students to use LLMs for Analytics\"\n110\t**What it was about:** A spring-piloted exercise teaching MBAs to use LLMs **discerningly** for analytics, rather than blindly copy-pasting problems into a model.\n111\t- Motivated by an Anthropic study finding AI assistance cut quiz mastery 17% versus coding by hand.\n112\t- A \"data cleaning\" assignment fed synthetic retail point-of-sale data to an LLM; students had to surface the silent cleaning choices (imputation, dropping rows, 999 codes) each model made.\n113\t- A second assignment on **model drift / MLOps** had students compare model accuracy versus business impact on recent data (MAE rose 4.19â†’5.86; RÂ² fell 0.77â†’0.53).\n114\t- Across ~5â€“6 sections, roughly half just copy-pasted from one LLM and only ~20% engaged deeply.\n115\t\n116\t**Notable takeaway:** Teaching fundamentals helps students know *what to ask* an LLM â€” though the models keep improving and may eventually erode that scaffolding.\n117\t\n118\t---\n119\t\n120\t## Afternoon\n121\t\n122\t### 2:00â€“2:25 Â· GenAI-driven oral assessments\n123\t\n124\t#### Panos Ipeirotis, NYU Stern\n125\t**What it was about:** A field report on a **voice-AI system that conducts and grades scalable oral exams**, deployed across two cohorts of an AI/ML Product Management course (demo: viva.stern.nyu.edu).\n126\t- Framed as a *measurement* problem (written work no longer proves thinking), not a cheating problem; oral exams work but don't scale (~2â€“3 faculty-days for a 36-student class).\n127\t- Two pipelines: a **voice-AI exam** (project + case discussion via ElevenLabs) feeding an **\"LLM council\"** where Claude, Gemini, and GPT grade independently, deliberate, then a Claude \"chair\" issues the final grade with transcript-cited evidence.\n128\t- Deliberation sharply raised inter-rater agreement; the lenient Gemini outlier forced the others to justify deductions. The external grade became a *mirror of teaching quality* (it correctly flagged a rushed A/B-testing unit).\n129\t- Cost â‰ˆ **$3 per 30-minute exam** (~11Â¢/student-minute). Failure modes (question-stacking, an over-aggressive cloned voice, non-randomized cases) taught: **constrain LLM behavior in code, not prompts.**\n130\t\n131\t**Notable takeaway:** Publishing the rubric and a practice agent in advance inverts incentives â€” rehearsing to explain your reasoning out loud *becomes* the learning. *(A focused standalone summary of this talk accompanies the shareable package.)*\n132\t\n133\t### 2:25â€“3:15 Â· Vibe coding and vibe analytics\n134\t\n135\t#### LÃ©onard Boussioux, Foster School of Business (UW)\n136\t**What it was about:** Teaching **\"vibe coding\"** through a build-first, passion-driven progression culminating in a public Gen-AI/agentic fair judged by industry alumni.\n137\t- Build-first structure (2Ã—5 three-hour sessions); fundamentals deferred until students have hands-on maturity.\n138\t- A four-level \"vibe coding ladder\": a Snake/Tetris game in ChatGPT/Claude â†’ a personal page in Lovable â†’ connecting Lovable to Claude Code/Codex â†’ vibe analytics with Streamlit.\n139\t- Demoed his own 100%-vibe-coded birding app (3,000 prompts; 1,000 users in three days) â€” candidly noting the backend was hackable, a hook into teaching **cybersecurity fundamentals**.\n140\t- Homework one: build a real website for a local/family business; ~75% of stakeholders loved it and ~20% of sites were adopted. He used embeddings to compare student sites for originality.\n141\t\n142\t**Notable takeaway:** Anchoring AI teaching in personal passion and visible, witnessed projects makes students build far more than a conventional assignment would.\n143\t\n144\t#### Rob Shumsky, Tuck School of Business (Dartmouth)\n145\t**What it was about:** Teaching **\"Predictive Analytics with Generative AI,\"** where students prompt an LLM to write and run Python for a traditional ML curriculum, organized by a three-part framework.\n146\t- Uses a custom RAG ChatGPT in an EDU environment with no coding assumed; data sets span health care, airline delays, and recidivism.\n147\t- A song-titled framework: **\"Don't Stop Believin'\"** (concepts to internalize AI-off, honor-system quizzes), **\"Higher Ground\"** (use GenAI to do more), and **\"Go Your Own Way\"** (where humans still beat GenAI).\n148\t- \"Higher Ground\" builds \"muscle memory\" via directive assignments, then a \"let the GPT go free\" task exposes failures (an LLM truncating real airline delays as outliers; only 30% got a column's average right).\n149\t- \"Go Your Own Way\" example: domain expertise on flight tail numbers raised RÂ² from ~0.10 to ~0.50 â€” something ChatGPT missed.\n150\t\n151\t**Notable takeaway:** The open question is whether the \"human advantage\" category is *shrinking* â€” which would raise hard questions for what programs teach.\n152\t\n153\t### 3:30â€“4:20 Â· Product management and vibe entrepreneurship\n154\t\n155\t#### Chris LaSala, Columbia Business School\n156\t**What it was about:** A new 6-week experiential course, **\"Prompt-to-Product: Building MVPs with AI,\"** teaching PMs to use AI/vibe-coding tools without \"building AI slop.\"\n157\t- AI compresses the entire PM lifecycle (problem â†’ MVP â†’ prototype â†’ validate â†’ iterate â†’ GTM), making product-building accessible to anyone (illustrated by a non-technical relative who vibe-coded a small paying product).\n158\t- Course design: 12 teams all solve **one real CBS problem** (helping the Career Management Center attract non-traditional recruiters) to shorten customer discovery.\n159\t- Students write a disciplined **PRD** in a living Google Doc with weekly faculty feedback â€” *they* define requirements, not the machine. Course ends in a Shark Tank judged by CBS administrators.\n160\t\n161\t**Notable takeaway:** The most future-proof PM uses AI to *think better and validate smarter* â€” valuing framing, contextual reasoning, and decision quality over documentation and delivery oversight.\n162\t\n163\t#### Brad Sturt, UIC Business *(no recording or slides posted)*\n164\tPart of the \"Product management and vibe entrepreneurship\" block; materials were not posted to the symposium site.\n165\t\n166\t### 4:20â€“5:00 Â· Mini-presentations â€” Part 2\n167\t\n168\t#### Song Ma, Yale School of Management â€” \"AI-Native Startups: A Course Built Around Agent CXOs\" *(no recording or slides posted)*\n169\tListed in the program; materials were not posted to the symposium site.\n170\t\n171\t#### Kyle Maclean, Ivey Business School â€” \"Hopeworks Foundation: An AI-Enabled Dynamic Case Experience\"\n172\t**What it was about:** A prototype **\"dynamic case\"** that replaces the one-shot case with an iterative experience where students consult for a fictional disaster-relief charity and must *request* data to reach a decision.\n173\t- Students act as consultants to HopeWorks Foundation, deciding whether home-page banners and/or email campaigns increase donations, starting from a brief and an initial dataset.\n174\t- The \"twist\": an intern character answers emailed questions and reveals new data, which flows into a built-in analytics studio for charts and regressions.\n175\t- **Faculty dashboards** let instructors see each student's conversations, the variables they accessed, and the models they ran.\n176\t- Piloted Fall 2025 across 10 sections (~780â€“800 students); praised for realism but many found it overwhelming.\n177\t\n178\t**Notable takeaway:** Like early film mimicking staged plays, AI cases shouldn't just bolt a chatbot onto a case â€” the dynamic, game-like medium is the point.\n179\t\n180\t#### Mattan Griffel, Columbia Business School â€” \"Teaching Entrepreneurship in the Age of AI\"\n181\t**What it was about:** A redesign of CBS's **\"Foundations of Entrepreneurship\"** showing how AI transforms nearly every stage of building a startup, taught through learning-by-doing.\n182\t- The cost to launch a startup has dropped ~10,000Ã— in 25 years (~$5M in 2000 â†’ ~$0â€“500 today); students built working apps in ~7 minutes each.\n183\t- AI introduces stage-specific risks: mistaking building for validating, premature scaling, loss of objectivity, tech debt, scope creep, and security.\n184\t- Introduced an **\"AI leverage matrix\"** (expertise Ã— stakes): let AI do it / tutor / accelerate / thought-partner.\n185\t- Demoed Lovable, Replit, Claude Code, and agentic startup \"skills\"; referenced Anthropic's \"Founders Playbook.\"\n186\t\n187\t**Notable takeaway:** The business plan is now even more useless â€” make students learn by doing, require AI use, but expect 3â€“4Ã— the output.\n188\t\n189\t#### Paul Canetti, Skej â€” \"Preparing our Students for an Agentic Workforce\"\n190\t**What it was about:** Three shifts students face entering an **agentic workforce**, drawn from his company building \"AI employees\" with email addresses, phone numbers, and Slack access.\n191\t- **\"Death of the IC\":** delegating to AI turns every individual contributor into a *manager of agents* â€” every entry-level job becomes a management job (scoping, assigning, reviewing, approving); one customer runs thousands of AI employees.\n192\t- **\"Not born AI-native\":** today's graduates grew up pre-AI and must *intentionally* learn to work with it.\n193\t- **\"Amazing at uncertainty\":** be honest that the future is unknowable; teach resourcefulness and frequent model-updating.\n194\t\n195\t**Notable takeaway:** Double down on what's certain â€” relationships and physical-world work â€” and send students off to be \"physically safe and professionally risky.\"\n196\t\n197\t---\n198\t\n199\t## Program at a glance\n200\t\n201\t| Time | Session | Speakers |\n202\t|---|---|---|\n203\t| 9:10â€“10:00 | Generative AI and the case method | Dan Wang (CBS); SÃ©bastien Martin (Kellogg) |\n204\t| 10:00â€“10:25 | Teaching Agentic AI | Ilan Lobel & Srikanth Jagabathula (NYU Stern) |\n205\t| 10:40â€“12:20 | GenAI in Business | Olivier Toubia & Malek Ben Sliman (CBS); Rama Ramakrishnan (MIT Sloan); Daniel Guetta (CBS); Kyle Jensen (Yale) |\n206\t| 12:20â€“12:50 | Mini-presentations â€” Part 1 | Mark Broadie (CBS); Luke Beasley (Meta); Thomas Lee (Berkeley Haas) |\n207\t| 2:00â€“2:25 | GenAI-driven oral assessments | Panos Ipeirotis (NYU Stern) |\n208\t| 2:25â€“3:15 | Vibe coding and vibe analytics | LÃ©onard Boussioux (Foster/UW); Rob Shumsky (Tuck) |\n209\t| 3:30â€“4:20 | Product management and vibe entrepreneurship | Chris LaSala (CBS); Brad Sturt (UIC) |\n210\t| 4:20â€“5:00 | Mini-presentations â€” Part 2 | Song Ma (Yale); Kyle Maclean (Ivey); Mattan Griffel (CBS); Paul Canetti (Skej) |\n211\t\n212\t*Source: post-conference materials at symposium.guetta.com. Recordings, slide decks, and verified transcripts for each recorded talk are included in this archive.*\n213\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8c1c2a55-4ddc-4a47-a802-c968e3709a87","uuid":"9afd4b72-f35a-46eb-88fe-b872537b4b53","timestamp":"2026-06-21T00:14:08.528Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\MBA Teaching AI\\Symposium 2026\\SUMMARY.md","content":"# The MBA Transformed? AI and Beyond â€” Symposium Summary\n### Columbia Business School Â· New York, NY Â· June 1, 2026\n\nA one-day symposium on how generative and agentic AI are reshaping business-school teaching â€” the case method, analytics, entrepreneurship, assessment, and the infrastructure behind it all. This document summarizes every session, in program order. Each summary is grounded in the speaker's own recording and slides.\n\n> **About these summaries.** They are drawn from verified transcripts of the session recordings: each talk was transcribed with Whisper large-v3 and independently cross-checked against Vimeo's auto-captions (â‰ˆ92â€“98% agreement across all talks), with proper nouns reconciled against the speakers' slides. Full recordings, slides, and transcripts accompany this document in the archive.\n\n---\n\n## The big picture â€” five threads that ran through the day\n\n1. **The case method is becoming interactive.** Instead of reading a static PDF, students now *talk to* AI â€” debating an AI interlocutor (Wang's CAiSEY), consulting AI-played characters in a living simulation (Martin; Maclean's HopeWorks), or being examined by a voice agent (Ipeirotis).\n2. **\"Agents are business processes.\"** Several speakers reframed agentic AI as systems-design and process-thinking rather than a tool â€” and argued the durable skill is deciding *where the human sits* in the workflow (Lobel & Jagabathula; Canetti).\n3. **Vibe coding makes everyone a builder.** Across many talks, MBAs build real, deployed apps with Lovable, Claude Code, Codex, Replit, Streamlit and Excel add-ins â€” shifting the skill from *writing* code to *specifying, verifying and iterating* (Broadie, Guetta, Boussioux, Shumsky, LaSala, Griffel).\n4. **Assessment is in crisis â€” and being reinvented.** When written work no longer proves thinking, faculty turn to AI-run oral exams (Ipeirotis), discernment exercises (Lee), honor-system \"AI-off\" quizzes (Shumsky) and dialogue-as-assignment platforms (Beasley's Socrat).\n5. **AI commoditizes \"good enough,\" so teaching must shift to differentiation and judgment.** A recurring thesis (sharpest in Toubia & Ben Sliman): since anyone can now produce adequate work, courses must teach students to differentiate themselves, exercise discernment, and do the things AI still can't.\n\n---\n\n## Morning\n\n### 9:10â€“10:00 Â· Generative AI and the case method\n\n#### Dan Wang, Columbia Business School\n**What it was about:** A demo of **CAiSEY** (caisey.me), a voice-first AI tool that immerses students in real-time discussion to deepen critical thinking and prepare them for case-method classes.\n- Live demo had a colleague role-play a \"mediocre\" student debating Netflix's content strategy (original vs. third-party licensing) against the AI.\n- CAiSEY uses an OpenAI voice model plus a system prompt supporting debate, brainstorm, and role-play modes, customized per course with instructor questions, materials, and custom instructions.\n- Scaled from a Spring 2025 pilot (275 students, 1 course) to **4,100+ students across 40+ courses and ~23â€“25 institutions**; valued as motivating, practical, and inclusive (ESL students, dyslexia/dysgraphia).\n- An under-review paper found voice discussions show greater semantic diversity than text (an indicator of cognitive flexibility); students start in text but migrate to voice.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** CAiSEY aims not to make learning more efficient but to add productive *friction* so students still struggle toward their own ideas.\n\n#### SÃ©bastien Martin, Kellogg School of Management\n**What it was about:** Reimagining the case study as an **interactive simulation** where students talk directly to AI-role-played characters instead of reading a static PDF.\n- Demo of an operations case on a school-district transportation budget crisis, where students consult characters like a superintendent and a CFO â€” each a custom GPT with role-specific prompts and its own data, who disagree with one another.\n- The format is adaptive, trains students to figure out the problem themselves, and is easy to edit via AI.\n- Spreading at Kellogg into negotiation, boardroom (multi-AI), and data-analysis classes; a ~60-instructor cross-school team is running an RCT to measure impact.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** It's never been easier for a professor to design a \"simulation,\" but the payoff hinges entirely on how deliberately the instructor sets up the learning experience.\n\n### 10:00â€“10:25 Â· Teaching Agentic AI\n\n#### Ilan Lobel & Srikanth Jagabathula, NYU Stern\n**What it was about:** How Stern built **\"Foundations of AI Agents,\"** a hands-on core course in its Tech MBA teaching systems-level thinking about agentic workflows.\n- Half-semester (six weeks, 3 hrs/week); first taught Spring 2026 with ~60 students and 120 waitlisted, scaling to 8 sections.\n- Two principles: **MBAs must be builders** (prototype, no-code) and **agents are business processes**; the arc runs Build â†’ Architect â†’ User-facing â†’ Enterprise/RAG â†’ Deploy â†’ Demo.\n- Teaching platform is **n8n** (a visual pedagogical device, \"like Excel for optimization\"); **Lovable** for front-ends.\n- Two lessons: focus on **where the human sits** (guardrail vs. bottleneck; automation vs. augmentation); and **\"don't do demos\"** â€” flashy demos bored students and failed the test of durable skill.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** Teaching AI *agents* differs from teaching AI â€” the durable skill is systems-level process thinking, especially deciding where humans belong in the workflow.\n\n### 10:40â€“12:20 Â· GenAI in Business â€” what it is, how it works, and how it can be used\n\n#### Olivier Toubia & Malek Ben Sliman, Columbia Business School\n**What it was about:** Lessons from teaching **\"Generative AI for Business\"** since fall 2023, organized around using GenAI as a source of *differentiation* rather than commoditization.\n- Serves four personas (users, consultants, developers, investors); the largest segment was tool users.\n- Structured as a tree: roots (foundations and the \"dark side\"), trees (building tools, incl. Python and deployment), forest (ecosystem, monetization, managing GenAI).\n- Core thesis: GenAI commoditizes \"good enough\" labor, so students must differentiate **vertically** (be the best with AI) and **horizontally** (be unique â€” build an \"AI identity\").\n- Students build a personal **RAG \"MBA Copilot\"** in ~30 minutes (300+ built) with GitHub/Pinecone/Vercel/GPT-4o, and contribute to \"The A(I) List,\" a crowdsourced tool-review platform.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** Since anyone can now produce \"good enough\" work, the mission is teaching students to use GenAI to *differentiate themselves* so people still pay for their work.\n\n#### Rama Ramakrishnan, MIT Sloan\n**What it was about:** Five years teaching **\"Hands-On Deep Learning\" (HODL)**, a code-first deep-learning elective for Sloan MBAs.\n- Philosophy: \"light on math, heavy on intuition,\" with students coding models themselves rather than calling a \"techie friend.\"\n- Wildly popular: the Spring 2026 section had the highest enrollment of all Sloan electives (170 registered, 100 waitlisted); the 2024 edition is on MIT OpenCourseWare.\n- Mixes slides, live coding in Google Colab, and unscripted live demos (e.g., students try to fool an image classifier).\n- Curriculum evolved to make **Transformers the platform**, adding LLM fine-tuning, RAG, agents-from-scratch, and diffusion models while dropping CNNs.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** Teach MBAs \"one level deeper than you think they need\" so they can do hard things and earn credibility with engineers.\n\n#### Daniel Guetta, Columbia Business School\n**What it was about:** A live demo of **XLKitLearn v2**, an Excel add-in that lets MBAs use advanced GenAI (LLMs, embeddings, agents) without learning to code from scratch.\n- Chose Excel over vibe coding to preserve structured, computer-like thinking (\"dragging a formula down\" teaches a loop without naming it).\n- Brings LLMs into Excel via `xkl.llm`, running prompts across multiple models with per-call cost reporting and guardrail comparisons; embeddings via `xkl.embed` (text is \"closest\" to its own author).\n- Agent tools (\"batteries included\"): send/receive texts, flight search, stock prices, and `runPython` in a sandbox; a server back-end manages per-student keys, quotas, and prompt caching for cost control.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** \"There is so much more to AI than a chatbot\" â€” Excel can turn non-coders into builders of embeddings- and agent-driven applications.\n\n#### Kyle Jensen, Yale School of Management *(slides only â€” not recorded)*\n**What it was about:** A fast, technical tour of **infrastructure for teaching with AI** â€” how to run your own teaching stack so agents (and students) have power without chaos.\n- **Write your own Canvas** (most universities disable the Canvas API; it's now \"vibe-codable\") for agent grading, point-in-time backups, and easy safety controls.\n- **GitHub Classroom + the `gh` CLI** for agent-friendly assignment distribution and auto-grading; **gateways/OpenRouter** for per-student LLM keys, budgets, and request inspection.\n- **Skills** (\"a Claude Code marketplace\") to give student/AI pairs *your taste* by default; **Ansible/OpenTofu** for reproducible infrastructure-as-code; **Tailscale** for private services.\n- **Meilisearch / full-text search** as \"the killer enabler\" of agentic AI (\"RAG is dead; use AI as a reasoning machine, not a fact database\"); **Playwright-CLI** to smoke-test apps; **guard models** to police inputs/outputs and redact PII.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** Own your teaching infrastructure â€” full-text search plus agent-friendly tooling beats bolting a chatbot onto someone else's LMS.\n\n### 12:20â€“12:50 Â· Mini-presentations â€” Part 1\n\n#### Mark Broadie, Columbia Business School â€” \"Using lovable.dev in Teaching\"\n**What it was about:** Using **Lovable** (an AI website builder) to teach the full analytics process â€” get data, build model, present results â€” and, for the first time, how to *manage a team of data scientists*.\n- Lovable writes scraping code for live data, writes analytical functions (replacing Excel add-ins), and builds interactive, hosted dashboards (React/Tailwind + Supabase, one-click publish, code you own).\n- A single naive prompt gets surprisingly far but is \"garbage\"; the real skill is **iterative refinement** to a polished product, then saving the final prompt as a reusable \"skill.\"\n- Examples: an NFL win-probability chart \"better than ESPN,\" NCAA-regionals map, sortable radar charts.\n- Stresses **verification**: build test suites (e.g., match R `glm` / Python `statsmodels` to 3 decimals) since students assume AI output is right.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** Lovable reaches the presentation/communication stage Excel never could, letting students learn to specify, verify, and iterate like managers of data scientists.\n\n#### Luke Beasley, Meta â€” \"Socrat: AI-Powered Learning for the Modern Classroom\"\n**What it was about:** **Socrat** turns AI dialogue itself into trackable assignments and gives instructors a data feedback loop on student understanding.\n- Addresses the \"data opportunity cost\": students already have hundreds of isolated AI conversations where struggling students stay invisible.\n- Instructors set up a class in minutes by uploading materials, yielding an LMS-style homepage and a class-grounded tutor.\n- Assignment types where *dialogue is the assignment*: Socratic dialogue, debate, teach-back, source analysis â€” each with an instructor-designed reasoning path.\n- A data layer surfaces strengths/weaknesses at assignment, student, and class levels.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** When AI dialogue becomes the assignment in one closed ecosystem, instructors finally see how every student thinks â€” including those who never raise a hand.\n\n#### Thomas Lee, UC Berkeley Haas â€” \"Teaching Students to use LLMs for Analytics\"\n**What it was about:** A spring-piloted exercise teaching MBAs to use LLMs **discerningly** for analytics, rather than blindly copy-pasting problems into a model.\n- Motivated by an Anthropic study finding AI assistance cut quiz mastery 17% versus coding by hand.\n- A \"data cleaning\" assignment fed synthetic retail point-of-sale data to an LLM; students had to surface the silent cleaning choices (imputation, dropping rows, 999 codes) each model made.\n- A second assignment on **model drift / MLOps** had students compare model accuracy versus business impact on recent data (MAE rose 4.19â†’5.86; RÂ² fell 0.77â†’0.53).\n- Across ~5â€“6 sections, roughly half just copy-pasted from one LLM and only ~20% engaged deeply.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** Teaching fundamentals helps students know *what to ask* an LLM â€” though the models keep improving and may eventually erode that scaffolding.\n\n---\n\n## Afternoon\n\n### 2:00â€“2:25 Â· GenAI-driven oral assessments\n\n#### Panos Ipeirotis, NYU Stern\n**What it was about:** A field report on a **voice-AI system that conducts and grades scalable oral exams**, deployed across two cohorts of an AI/ML Product Management course (demo: viva.stern.nyu.edu).\n- Framed as a *measurement* problem (written work no longer proves thinking), not a cheating problem; oral exams work but don't scale (~2â€“3 faculty-days for a 36-student class).\n- Two pipelines: a **voice-AI exam** (project + case discussion via ElevenLabs) feeding an **\"LLM council\"** where Claude, Gemini, and GPT grade independently, deliberate, then a Claude \"chair\" issues the final grade with transcript-cited evidence.\n- Deliberation sharply raised inter-rater agreement; the lenient Gemini outlier forced the others to justify deductions. The external grade became a *mirror of teaching quality* (it correctly flagged a rushed A/B-testing unit).\n- Cost â‰ˆ **$3 per 30-minute exam** (~11Â¢/student-minute). Failure modes (question-stacking, an over-aggressive cloned voice, non-randomized cases) taught: **constrain LLM behavior in code, not prompts.**\n\n**Notable takeaway:** Publishing the rubric and a practice agent in advance inverts incentives â€” rehearsing to explain your reasoning out loud *becomes* the learning. *(A focused standalone summary of this talk accompanies the shareable package.)*\n\n### 2:25â€“3:15 Â· Vibe coding and vibe analytics\n\n#### LÃ©onard Boussioux, Foster School of Business (UW)\n**What it was about:** Teaching **\"vibe coding\"** through a build-first, passion-driven progression culminating in a public Gen-AI/agentic fair judged by industry alumni.\n- Build-first structure (2Ã—5 three-hour sessions); fundamentals deferred until students have hands-on maturity.\n- A four-level \"vibe coding ladder\": a Snake/Tetris game in ChatGPT/Claude â†’ a personal page in Lovable â†’ connecting Lovable to Claude Code/Codex â†’ vibe analytics with Streamlit.\n- Demoed his own 100%-vibe-coded birding app (3,000 prompts; 1,000 users in three days) â€” candidly noting the backend was hackable, a hook into teaching **cybersecurity fundamentals**.\n- Homework one: build a real website for a local/family business; ~75% of stakeholders loved it and ~20% of sites were adopted. He used embeddings to compare student sites for originality.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** Anchoring AI teaching in personal passion and visible, witnessed projects makes students build far more than a conventional assignment would.\n\n#### Rob Shumsky, Tuck School of Business (Dartmouth)\n**What it was about:** Teaching **\"Predictive Analytics with Generative AI,\"** where students prompt an LLM to write and run Python for a traditional ML curriculum, organized by a three-part framework.\n- Uses a custom RAG ChatGPT in an EDU environment with no coding assumed; data sets span health care, airline delays, and recidivism.\n- A song-titled framework: **\"Don't Stop Believin'\"** (concepts to internalize AI-off, honor-system quizzes), **\"Higher Ground\"** (use GenAI to do more), and **\"Go Your Own Way\"** (where humans still beat GenAI).\n- \"Higher Ground\" builds \"muscle memory\" via directive assignments, then a \"let the GPT go free\" task exposes failures (an LLM truncating real airline delays as outliers; only 30% got a column's average right).\n- \"Go Your Own Way\" example: domain expertise on flight tail numbers raised RÂ² from ~0.10 to ~0.50 â€” something ChatGPT missed.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** The open question is whether the \"human advantage\" category is *shrinking* â€” which would raise hard questions for what programs teach.\n\n### 3:30â€“4:20 Â· Product management and vibe entrepreneurship\n\n#### Chris LaSala, Columbia Business School\n**What it was about:** A new 6-week experiential course, **\"Prompt-to-Product: Building MVPs with AI,\"** teaching PMs to use AI/vibe-coding tools without \"building AI slop.\"\n- AI compresses the entire PM lifecycle (problem â†’ MVP â†’ prototype â†’ validate â†’ iterate â†’ GTM), making product-building accessible to anyone (illustrated by a non-technical relative who vibe-coded a small paying product).\n- Course design: 12 teams all solve **one real CBS problem** (helping the Career Management Center attract non-traditional recruiters) to shorten customer discovery.\n- Students write a disciplined **PRD** in a living Google Doc with weekly faculty feedback â€” *they* define requirements, not the machine. Course ends in a Shark Tank judged by CBS administrators.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** The most future-proof PM uses AI to *think better and validate smarter* â€” valuing framing, contextual reasoning, and decision quality over documentation and delivery oversight.\n\n#### Brad Sturt, UIC Business *(no recording or slides posted)*\nPart of the \"Product management and vibe entrepreneurship\" block; materials were not posted to the symposium site.\n\n### 4:20â€“5:00 Â· Mini-presentations â€” Part 2\n\n#### Song Ma, Yale School of Management â€” \"AI-Native Startups: A Course Built Around Agent CXOs\" *(no recording or slides posted)*\nListed in the program; materials were not posted to the symposium site.\n\n#### Kyle Maclean, Ivey Business School â€” \"Hopeworks Foundation: An AI-Enabled Dynamic Case Experience\"\n**What it was about:** A prototype **\"dynamic case\"** that replaces the one-shot case with an iterative experience where students consult for a fictional disaster-relief charity and must *request* data to reach a decision.\n- Students act as consultants to HopeWorks Foundation, deciding whether home-page banners and/or email campaigns increase donations, starting from a brief and an initial dataset.\n- The \"twist\": an intern character answers emailed questions and reveals new data, which flows into a built-in analytics studio for charts and regressions.\n- **Faculty dashboards** let instructors see each student's conversations, the variables they accessed, and the models they ran.\n- Piloted Fall 2025 across 10 sections (~780â€“800 students); praised for realism but many found it overwhelming.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** Like early film mimicking staged plays, AI cases shouldn't just bolt a chatbot onto a case â€” the dynamic, game-like medium is the point.\n\n#### Mattan Griffel, Columbia Business School â€” \"Teaching Entrepreneurship in the Age of AI\"\n**What it was about:** A redesign of CBS's **\"Foundations of Entrepreneurship\"** showing how AI transforms nearly every stage of building a startup, taught through learning-by-doing.\n- The cost to launch a startup has dropped ~10,000Ã— in 25 years (~$5M in 2000 â†’ ~$0â€“500 today); students built working apps in ~7 minutes each.\n- AI introduces stage-specific risks: mistaking building for validating, premature scaling, loss of objectivity, tech debt, scope creep, and security.\n- Introduced an **\"AI leverage matrix\"** (expertise Ã— stakes): let AI do it / tutor / accelerate / thought-partner.\n- Demoed Lovable, Replit, Claude Code, and agentic startup \"skills\"; referenced Anthropic's \"Founders Playbook.\"\n\n**Notable takeaway:** The business plan is now even more useless â€” make students learn by doing, require AI use, but expect 3â€“4Ã— the output.\n\n#### Paul Canetti, Skej â€” \"Preparing our Students for an Agentic Workforce\"\n**What it was about:** Three shifts students face entering an **agentic workforce**, drawn from his company building \"AI employees\" with email addresses, phone numbers, and Slack access.\n- **\"Death of the IC\":** delegating to AI turns every individual contributor into a *manager of agents* â€” every entry-level job becomes a management job (scoping, assigning, reviewing, approving); one customer runs thousands of AI employees.\n- **\"Not born AI-native\":** today's graduates grew up pre-AI and must *intentionally* learn to work with it.\n- **\"Amazing at uncertainty\":** be honest that the future is unknowable; teach resourcefulness and frequent model-updating.\n\n**Notable takeaway:** Double down on what's certain â€” relationships and physical-world work â€” and send students off to be \"physically safe and professionally risky.\"\n\n---\n\n## Program at a glance\n\n| Time | Session | Speakers |\n|---|---|---|\n| 9:10â€“10:00 | Generative AI and the case method | Dan Wang (CBS); SÃ©bastien Martin (Kellogg) |\n| 10:00â€“10:25 | Teaching Agentic AI | Ilan Lobel & Srikanth Jagabathula (NYU Stern) |\n| 10:40â€“12:20 | GenAI in Business | Olivier Toubia & Malek Ben Sliman (CBS); Rama Ramakrishnan (MIT Sloan); Daniel Guetta (CBS); Kyle Jensen (Yale) |\n| 12:20â€“12:50 | Mini-presentations â€” Part 1 | Mark Broadie (CBS); Luke Beasley (Meta); Thomas Lee (Berkeley Haas) |\n| 2:00â€“2:25 | GenAI-driven oral assessments | Panos Ipeirotis (NYU Stern) |\n| 2:25â€“3:15 | Vibe coding and vibe analytics | LÃ©onard Boussioux (Foster/UW); Rob Shumsky (Tuck) |\n| 3:30â€“4:20 | Product management and vibe entrepreneurship | Chris LaSala (CBS); Brad Sturt (UIC) |\n| 4:20â€“5:00 | Mini-presentations â€” Part 2 | Song Ma (Yale); Kyle Maclean (Ivey); Mattan Griffel (CBS); Paul Canetti (Skej) |\n\n*Source: post-conference materials at symposium.guetta.com. Recordings, slide decks, and verified transcripts for each recorded talk are included in this archive.*\n","numLines":213,"startLine":1,"totalLines":213}}}
